label: Raw Parcel Feature
description: >-
  ########################################################################
  # THIS EDGE IS THE ONE THAT DOES NOT FIT. READ THE COMMENT.            #
  ########################################################################

  What blue-ribbon actually has here is:

      interface RawParcelFeature {
        properties: Record<string, unknown>;
        geometry: { type: string; coordinates: unknown };
      }

  `Record<string, unknown>` is not an accident or laziness. It is the entire
  reason the adapter layer exists. Five counties return five different field
  names for the same concept -- Osceola `PIN`/`OWNER`/`PROPADD`/`UNIT`,
  Iosco `TaxID`, Otsego `parcelid`, Manistee `BSA_PIN`, Roscommon `PIN` with
  shapefile-truncated siblings like `c_Parcel_I` and `ParcelMa_3` that
  05_SPIKE_FINDINGS.md says "need a decode pass". The shape is DISCOVERED by
  querying a live server, not declared. Some of it is discovered wrong and
  corrected months later.

  weir says an edge is "the complete description of what crosses a wire". An
  ArcGIS FeatureServer response is a wire whose contents I genuinely cannot
  describe in advance, and if I could, I would not want to -- the whole point
  of `normalize()` is that the mess stops at one place.

  WHAT I ACTUALLY WANT, and weir has no way to say:

      fields:
        featureJson:
          type: opaque          # <-- does not exist
          schema: unchecked     # <-- does not exist
          note: "asserted by normalizeParcel, not by the membrane"

  i.e. an edge that is explicitly OUTSIDE the schema guarantee, so that a
  reader can see the guarantee stops here rather than believing a `utf8` that
  is lying to them.

  WHAT I DID INSTEAD: stuffed the whole ArcGIS feature into one utf8 field as
  a JSON string. This parses and it is a lie. The membrane will happily assert
  `featureJson` is a string of length < 2000000 and tell me nothing. Every
  guarantee weir advertises -- schema assertion, structural hashing, typed
  generators for property testing -- is inert across this one hop, and this
  one hop is where every real bug in blue-ribbon's adapter layer has been.

  THE ALTERNATIVE I REJECTED: fold fetch and normalize into a single effect
  node, so the untyped blob never becomes an edge. That is worse. `normalize()`
  is pure, is the interesting logic, and is the one part of the adapter layer
  with real unit tests (apps/etl/test/counties/*.test.ts, 5 files). Putting it
  inside a host-supplied effect handler moves it PAST the acceptance gate --
  weir would have taken my best-tested code and made it untestable.
fields:
  county:
    type: utf8
    label: County
    description: Which county's adapter must interpret the payload below
    nullable: false
    validations:
      minLength: 4
      maxLength: 20
  sourceTrust:
    type: utf8
    label: Source Trust
    description: >-
      FRICTION (#5): this field exists only because the oneOf decision made by
      `routeCounty` is erased the moment both branches converge on this edge.
      weir's pitch is "a decision becomes a type, so the consumer's input type
      IS the proof the decision was made". That holds right up until two
      branches produce the same edge -- and converging is the normal case in
      ETL, because the point of an adapter is that downstream stops caring.

      To keep it as a type I would need RawParcelFeatureDirect and
      RawParcelFeatureProxied, and then normalizeParcel takes anyOf of two
      edges, and every node below it does too. The type survives only by
      duplicating the rest of the graph.

      So: back to a string field, checked by nobody, exactly as blue-ribbon
      has it today. weir gained me nothing here.
    nullable: false
    enumValues:
      - verified
      - aggregator
  featureJson:
    type: utf8
    label: Feature JSON
    description: >-
      The entire ArcGIS GeoJSON feature, serialized. See the edge description:
      this is a hole in the type system, deliberately marked.
    nullable: false
    validations:
      minLength: 2
      maxLength: 2000000
